{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_003", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 1, "page_end": 1, "char_count": 600, "text": "1 Orthopedic Department, San Gerardo Hospital, University o f Milano-Bicocca, Monza, Italy, 2 Department of Medicine and Surgery, University of Milano-Bicocca, Monza, Italy, 3 Department of Paediatric Orthopedic Surgery, University H ospital Grenoble-Alpes, University Grenoble-Alpes, Grenoble, Fr ance, 4 Transalpine Center of Pediatric Sports Medicine and Surger y, University of Milano-Bicocca, Monza, Italy, 5 Hospital Couple Enfant, Grenoble, France, 6 School of Physical and Occupational Therapy, McGill University, MontrÃ©al, QC, Ca nada, 7 Department of Kinesiology, University of Connecticut,"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_004", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 1, "page_end": 1, "char_count": 636, "text": "Storrs, CT , United States, 8 Department of Physical Therapy, Waldron College of Health a nd Human Services, Radford University, Roanoke, VA, United States Even though ankle sprains are among the most frequent muscul oskeletal injuries seen in emergency departments, management of these injurie s continues to lack standardization. Our objective was to carry out an umbrella review of systematic reviews to collect the most effective evidence-based treat ments and to point out the state-of-the-art management for this injury. PubMed, Scop us, Web of Science, and the Cochrane library were searched from January 2000 to Septemb er 2020."}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_005", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 1, "page_end": 1, "char_count": 629, "text": "After removing duplicates and applying the eligibility criteria, based on titles and abstracts, 32 studies were screened. At the end of the process, 24 articles were inc luded in this umbrella review with a mean score of 7.7/11 on the AMSTAR quality asses sment tool. We found evidence supporting the effectiveness of non-surgical tre atment in managing acute ankle sprain; moreover, functional treatment seems to be prefera ble to immobilization. We also found evidence supporting the use of paracetamol or opioids as effective alternatives to non-steroidal anti-inï¬‚ammatory drugs to reduce pain. Furt hermore, we found evidence"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_006", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 1, "page_end": 1, "char_count": 619, "text": "supporting the effectiveness of manipulative and supervis ed exercise therapy to prevent re-injury and restore ankle dorsiï¬‚exion. Keywords: rehabilitation, treatment, management, acute, ank le, sprain, injury INTRODUCTION During sports activities, especially indoor sports ( 1) (e.g., volleyball, basketball, and dance), acute ankle sprains are very common, with considerable diagnostic al and treatment costs for healthcare systems and high socioeconomic impact due to absenteeism fro m work ( 2). Epidemiological data have shown that âˆ¼ 80% of individuals will suï¬€er an ankle sprain during their lifeti me (3, 4). This"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_007", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 1, "page_end": 2, "char_count": 648, "text": "injury is more frequent in women than in men; likewise, children are more aï¬€ected than adolescents and adults: the progressive development of coordination patterns and neuromuscular control plays a protective role (5â€“8). Gaddi et al. Acute Ankle Sprain Management Less than 15% of acute ankle sprains are coupled with a fracture of the ankle or foot, suggesting that the damage mai nly concerns soft tissues. Recently, Romero-Morales et al. (9) showed a decreased thickness of the plantar fascia in individuals wit h lateral ankle sprain when compared with healthy subjects. X-r ays are required in 77â€“99% of cases (10, 11). Based on the anatomical"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_008", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 2, "page_end": 2, "char_count": 641, "text": "classiï¬cation, we can identify three types of ankle sprain patterns, namely, lateral, syndesmotic, and medial. Ankle sprains invo lve the lateral ligament group ( âˆ¼ 85%), because it is less resistant to load, so it is easier to injure compared with the others ligament groups ( 12). The lateral ankle ligament is composed of the anterior taloï¬bular ligament (ATFL), the calcaneoï¬b ular ligament (CFL), and the posterior taloï¬bular ligament (PTFL ) (10). The ATFL is the weakest and when it is damaged it causes an antero-posterior instability, while the involvement of t he CFL turns into an inversion instability. In emergency departmen ts,"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_009", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 2, "page_end": 2, "char_count": 615, "text": "a clinical classiï¬cation is more valuable. We can divide acu te ankle sprain into three diï¬€erent grades ( Table 1): grade I injuries ligaments are only stretched without macroscopic tearing; g rade II injuries show partial tear of ligaments, frequently with a complete tear of the ATFL and an additional partial tear of the CFL; and ï¬nally grade III injuries are associated with complet e tear of ligaments with disruption of both the ATFL and CFL, capsular tear could be associated. Although several studies on acute ankle sprain are available , there is a considerable variety of classiï¬cations, follow-u p times,"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_010", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 2, "page_end": 2, "char_count": 635, "text": "treatments, outcome measures, and endpoints. Ankle sprain treatment ranges from physical therapy [e.g., orthoses, functional exercise, strength, and endurance ( 13)] to pharmacology support and orthopedic surgery. Thus, it is diï¬ƒcult to propose a standar d algorithm to manage and treat an acute ankle sprain when it occurs in the emergency department. Moreover, a systematic review of published clinical practice guidelines for the trea tment of acute lateral ankle sprains revealed that their quality was poor ( 14). Therefore, this study reviews literature on the diï¬€erent treatments proposed for acute ankle sprain. Speciï¬cally, by"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_011", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 2, "page_end": 2, "char_count": 598, "text": "means of an umbrella review of systematic reviews, we analyze and describe the state-of-the-art management of this injur y. MATERIALS AND METHODS Focused Question Based This study is an umbrella review of systematic reviews. Following the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Review and Meta- analysis (PRISMA) ( 15) guidelines, we developed a speciï¬c foreground question that focused on the management of ankle sprains. The following PICO was formulated: Population : adults with acute ankle sprain; Intervention and Comparison: conservative and surgical treatments; Outcomes: pain, swel ling,"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_012", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 2, "page_end": 2, "char_count": 626, "text": "range of motion, instability, function, reinjury rate, and return Abbreviations: ATFL, anterior taloï¬bular ligament; CFL, calcaneoï¬bular ligament; PTFL, posterior taloï¬bular ligament; PRISMA, Preferred Report ing Items for Systematic Review and Meta-analysis; RICE, rest, ice, compression, elevation; NSAIDs, non-steroidal anti-inï¬‚ammatory drugs; RTS, re turn to sport; ROM, range of mobility. to sport (see Section Search Strategy and Study Selection for additional details). Eligibility Criteria We only considered articles that satisfy the following elig ibility criteria: (i) the study must be a systematic review, with or"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_013", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 2, "page_end": 2, "char_count": 641, "text": "without a meta-analysis; (ii) it must assess the suitability of an intervention for the treatment or prevention of ankle sprains; and (iii) the population examined must not include chronic ankle instability. We have excluded studies dealing exclusively w ith epidemiology, etiopathogenesis, and diagnosis of ankle sprains. We have also excluded studies concerning pediatric patients. W e have included all types of treatments. Search Strategy and Study Selection We conducted a comprehensive literature search on PubMed, Scopus, Web of Science, and the Cochrane library from 2000 up to September 2020. We included only systematic reviews and"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_014", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 2, "page_end": 2, "char_count": 625, "text": "meta-analyses in order to analyze the highest level of eviden ce and to increase the external validity of the present study. We developed our research using the following keywords: (a) â€œankleâ€ AND â€œsprainâ€; (b) â€œankle sprainâ€ AND â€œmanagementâ€; (c) â€œankle sprainâ€ AND â€œtreatmentâ€. No ï¬elds in the search engines were speciï¬ed in order to expand the research (e.g., in PubMed the word â€œtreatmentâ€ was translated by the search engine as â€œtherapeuticsâ€ [MH] OR â€œtherapeuticsâ€ [All Fields] OR â€œtreatmentsâ€ [All Fields] OR â€œtherapyâ€ [Subheading] OR â€œtherapyâ€ [All Fields] OR â€œtreatmentâ€ [All Fields] OR â€œtreatmentâ€™sâ€ [All Fields])."}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_015", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 2, "page_end": 2, "char_count": 630, "text": "As a result, we found 3,500 cita tions. Diï¬€erent authors screened all the articles found, ï¬rst remov ing duplicates, and then applying the eligibility criteria based o n titles and abstracts; the 32 remaining studies were checked a second time by reading them and applying eligibility criteria t o identify a list of relevant articles. Any disagreement was r esolved by discussion among all the authors ( Figure 1). Another eight studies were then removed, leaving a total of 24 articles for this umbrella review. Regarding treatments, we considered both conservative and surgical strategies. Among non-surgical intervention s, we"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_016", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 2, "page_end": 2, "char_count": 623, "text": "reported acute treatment [e.g., RICE (rest, ice, compression, elevation) therapy, immobilization, taping, and bracing], d rugs [including NSAIDs (non-steroidal anti-inï¬‚ammatory drugs) and other medications], rehabilitation and manual therapy (e.g ., proprioceptive, coordination and strength training, supervis ed exercises, and manipulative therapy), and complementary therapies (e.g., acupuncture and electrical stimulation). W e selected these categories according to a literature review . Diï¬€erent outcomes have been assessed, including pain, swelli ng, range of motion, instability, function, and reinjury rate. In our"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_017", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 2, "page_end": 3, "char_count": 610, "text": "study, we also focused on return to sport. Methodological Study Quality Assessment Two authors (DG and MC) independently assessed the quality of the systematic reviews using the AMSTAR tool ( 16). Any disagreement was resolved by group discussion and consensus. Consistent with the methods used by Doherty et al., we decided to assess the review as â€œhighâ€ or â€œlowâ€ quality, base d Frontiers in Medicine | www.frontiersin.org 2 July 2022 | Volume 9 | Article 868474 Gaddi et al. Acute Ankle Sprain Management TABLE 1 | Classiï¬cation of lateral ankle sprain based on increasing li gamentous damage and morbidity."}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_018", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 3, "page_end": 4, "char_count": 616, "text": "Grade Hematoma/swelling/pain Anterior drawer test T alar til t test Anatomic lesion Stability I Positive Negative Negative Incomplete tear of ATFL Stable II Positive Positive Negative Complete tear of ATFL, incompl ete tear of CFL Unstable III Positive Positive Positive Complete tear of ATFL, complet e tear of CFL Unstable ATFL, anterior taloï¬bular ligament; CFL, calcaneoï¬bular ligamen t (12). FIGURE 1 | PRISMA ï¬‚ow diagram. Frontiers in Medicine | www.frontiersin.org 3 July 2022 | Volume 9 | Article 868474 Gaddi et al. Acute Ankle Sprain Management on a score of 7/11 on the AMSTAR quality rating. This quality"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_019", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 4, "page_end": 4, "char_count": 641, "text": "threshold was used to conduct the best evidence synthesis of reviews ( 17). RESUL TS Study Selection We performed our research following PRISMA guidelines ( 15), as shown in Figure 1. In the beginning, we collected 3,500 studies. At the end of the process, only 24 systematic reviews met the eligibility criteria and were therefore included, w ith a mean AMSTAR score of 7.7/11 ( Table 2 shows the quality of the selected articles, ï¬rst author, and year of publication). All the studies considered investigated treatment strateg ies for acute ankle sprains. Six reviews investigated speciï¬call y acute treatment ( 18â€“23) (AMSTAR range 3â€“11)."}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_020", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 4, "page_end": 4, "char_count": 626, "text": "Two reviews evaluated drug therapy ( 24, 25) (AMSTAR range 8â€“11). Seven reviews evaluated rehabilitation and manual therapy ( 26â€“ 32) (AMSTAR range 5â€“10). Two reviews evaluated surgical treatment (33, 34) (AMSTAR range 6â€“9). Three reviews analyzed other treatments ( 35â€“37) (AMSTAR range 6â€“10). Finally, four reviews considered return to sport ( 38â€“41) (AMSTAR range 4â€“ 11). Table 3 summarizes the results and conclusions of included studies, grouped based on the intervention type. While severa l studies could have been assigned to more than one strategy, e ach review was noted once in Table 3, and the assignment was based"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_021", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 4, "page_end": 4, "char_count": 627, "text": "on the primary focus of the review. Acute Treatment Bleakley et al. ( 23) (AMSTAR 6) included 22 studies involving 1,469 participants to analyze the eï¬€ectiveness of cryotherapy t o manage acute soft-tissue damage. Concerning acute ankle sprain, there is marginal evidence that cryotherapy is eï¬€ective if it i s added to exercises during the management of the early stages of this injury. They do not ï¬nd consensus about cryotherapy duration and the method with which ice should be used ( 23). Jones and Amendola ( 18) (AMSTAR 3) included 9 studies involving 920 subjects to compare immobilization to early functional treatment."}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_022", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 4, "page_end": 4, "char_count": 647, "text": "They reported that functional treatm ent allows earlier return to sport and to work, while there is no substantial diï¬€erence concerning instability and re-injur y rate, although studies seem to favor early functional treatment. Kemler et al. ( 19) (AMSTAR 7) included nine studies involving 1,250 patients to compare braces to other functiona l treatment types. They found no diï¬€erences concerning time to return to pre-injury activities, time to reduce symptoms, re- injury, and joint instability rates, but it seems that brace s have better functional outcomes using the Foot and Ankle Outcome Score and Karlsson scoring scale. Kerkhoï¬€s et al. ("}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_023", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 4, "page_end": 4, "char_count": 641, "text": "20) (AMSTAR 10) included 22 studies involving 2,157 participants to analyze immobilization as a treatment for acute ankle sprains. They found that functiona l treatment has better results considering time to return to pr e- injury activities, swelling reduction, joint stiï¬€ness, and subjective and objective joint instability. They did not ï¬nd any diï¬€eren ces for recurrence or pain. Ortega-Avila et al. ( 21) (AMSTAR 11) included 20 studies, involving 2,236 subjects, to demonstrate that conservative treatment (e.g., RICE, cryotherapy, exercise, and manual therapy) eï¬€ectively manages pain and functional recovery. van den Bekerom et al. ("}
