{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_048", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 7, "page_end": 8, "char_count": 605, "text": "motion, and strength. RTS should be guided by static balance, patient-reported outcomes, range of motion, and strength. Frontiers in Medicine | www.frontiersin.org 7 July 2022 | Volume 9 | Article 868474 Gaddi et al. Acute Ankle Sprain Management Park et al. ( 37) (AMSTAR 9) included 17 studies involving 1,820 patients to examine the eï¬€ectiveness of acupuncture to reduce patientsâ€™ global symptoms and pain, especially as an add-on treatment. However, they do not recommend using this practice due to the limited number of studies and insuï¬ƒcient high-quality evidence. Return to Sport Al Bimani et al. ("}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_049", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 8, "page_end": 8, "char_count": 595, "text": "38) (AMSTAR 11) included 14 studies involving 1,142 patients to ï¬nd a consensus about inï¬‚uencing factors fo r return to play after an acute ankle sprain is treated conservatively. According to these authors, many factors should be considered as inï¬‚uential factors in return to play. Tassignon et al. ( 39) (AMSTAR 7) failed to ï¬nd studies that establish algorithms to deï¬ne the process to return to sport (RTS) for patients with a lateral ankle sprain, so they proposed variables that could be used for criteria-based RTS decision paradigm. V ancolen et al. ( 40) (AMSTAR 8) included 10 studies,"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_050", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 8, "page_end": 8, "char_count": 616, "text": "involving 333 patients to evaluate the rate of RTS after a syndesmotic ankle injury. According to the authors, most patients can return to sport at the preinjury level in both non- operative and operative treatment groups. AMSTAR 4 included 11 studies to ï¬nd consensus among expert opinions about items that have to be considered to develop an RTS criterion for the management of lateral ankle sprain. They found several important criteria, including sport-speci ï¬c movement, static balance, patient-reported outcomes, range of motion, and strength. DISCUSSION Despite the fact that acute ankle sprains are among the"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_051", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 8, "page_end": 8, "char_count": 649, "text": "most commonly seen injuries in the emergency department, a standardized protocol for its management has not been established. Furthermore, the heterogeneity of intervent ions and outcomes examined in this revision, and the range in quality o f these studies make it diï¬ƒcult to propose a standard algorithm f or acute ankle sprain management. Based on this umbrella review , non-surgical treatment is eï¬€ective to manage acute ankle sprains, and functional treatment seems to be superior when compared to immobilization. Moreover, paracetamol and opioids are as eï¬€ective as NSAIDs in reducing pain, thus they represent an alternative treatment option."}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_052", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 8, "page_end": 8, "char_count": 615, "text": "Manipulative and exercise the rapy could be also recommended, especially during the initial recovery phase, to prevent reinjury, and to restore dorsiï¬‚exion. Acute Treatment After an acute ankle sprain, during the ï¬rst few days (48â€“ 72 h), the most used treatment protocol is RICE therapy, which consists of a combination of rest, ice, compression, an d elevation. Rest reduces the metabolic tissue demand, decrea sing the amount of blood circulating in the damaged area. Ice indu ces vasoconstriction, it brings down the temperature, diminish ing the metabolic rate of cells. It also ï¬ghts against exudate formation"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_053", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 8, "page_end": 8, "char_count": 629, "text": "and hemorrhaging, which are responsible for the swelling. Finally, ice has an analgesic role, which is also used in the rehabilitation phase in order to facilitate exercises. Compr ession limits the swelling and stops hemorrhaging, while elevation improves lymph drainage and venous circulation ( 22, 23). Although several guidelines promote this approach, there are few studies that demonstrate the eï¬€ectiveness of this therapy , in part due to several diï¬ƒculties that exist when researchers try to create a study protocol ( 22). For example, most patients apply ice before going to the hospital and there are many diï¬€erent ways"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_054", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 8, "page_end": 8, "char_count": 625, "text": "to use cryotherapy, including mode, duration, and frequency of application ( 23). After the acute medical care, various treatments exist to manage the acute ankle sprain. Nevertheless, they can be classiï¬ed into three main groups, namely, (1) conservative or conventional, which includes immobilization with cast or s plint, (2) functional, which consists of early mobilization added to external supports, such as tapes, elastic bandages, and braces , in order to protect the joint from the risk of re-injury, as well as coordination training, and (3) surgery ( 20). According to our revision, functional treatment represents"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_055", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 8, "page_end": 8, "char_count": 634, "text": "a better choice than conservative treatment: fewer patients suï¬€er from swelling, ROM limitations, joint stiï¬€ness, and join t instability. Moreover, patients are more satisï¬ed, and they r eturn earlier to their pre-injury work and sports activities; thus, t he associated social costs could also be reduced ( 18â€“21). As far as recommending a functional treatment as the ï¬rst choice to manage an acute ankle sprain, there are few studies that compar e diï¬€erent types of external supports, even if it seems that some types of braces could be superior based on patient outcomes ( 19). We, therefore, recommend that more studies are needed"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_056", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 8, "page_end": 8, "char_count": 648, "text": "to understand which types of external support to use with functional treatment. Drugs To reduce swelling, reduce pain, and decrease the time to retu rn to work, NSAIDs are the most commonly used medications in the treatment of ankle sprains, as they have both analgesic and anti-inï¬‚ammatory eï¬€ects. Swelling and pain are mediated by th e inï¬‚ammation process, which is also a part of healing; NSAIDs, however, can impair this process. The most common side eï¬€ect is gastrointestinal bleeding, but they can cause other probl ems such as bronchospasm or renal failure. The advent of COX- 2 selective NSAIDs has recently decreased gastrointestina l side"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_057", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 8, "page_end": 8, "char_count": 637, "text": "eï¬€ects at the cost of higher cardiovascular risk ( 24). NSAIDs are usually prescribed in an oral or topical form, which are both more eï¬€ective than a placebo. However, we have not found studies comparing the two diï¬€erent formulations ( 25). Other drugs, customarily used, are paracetamol and opioids, alone or combined with each other, which does not have an anti- inï¬‚ammatory eï¬€ect. Opioids act centrally and peripherally on their speciï¬c receptors, while the mechanism of paracetamol is not fully clear, although it seems to involve several centr al pathways, including prostaglandin, serotonergic, nitric oxi de, and cannabinoid ones."}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_058", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 8, "page_end": 9, "char_count": 594, "text": "Paracetamol is hepatotoxic, while opioi ds are known to cause nausea, respiratory depression, sedation, vomiting, constipation, and dysphoria. Regarding pain, there i s no evidence of a diï¬€erence between paracetamol and NSAIDs; however, paracetamol seems to have fewer gastrointestinal side eï¬€ects. Considering pain, NSAIDs and opioids would be Frontiers in Medicine | www.frontiersin.org 8 July 2022 | Volume 9 | Article 868474 Gaddi et al. Acute Ankle Sprain Management equivalent, but NSAIDs would have fewer gastrointestinal a nd neurological side eï¬€ects. NSAIDs seem to reduce swelling and"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_059", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 9, "page_end": 9, "char_count": 612, "text": "help regain function better than paracetamol and opioids, but according to our review, we need more studies to conï¬rm these results with a higher grade of certainty ( 24). We recommend the use of paracetamol or opioids as eï¬€ective alternatives to NSAID s for pain reduction. More studies are needed that focus on the impact of COX-2 selective NSAIDs and diï¬€erent pharmaceutical formulations of these drugs. Rehabilitation and Manual Therapy Several ankle sprain rehabilitation programs are available with diï¬€erent methods and exercises, practiced alone or in combination with other speciï¬c treatments. Functional"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_060", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 9, "page_end": 9, "char_count": 629, "text": "treatment is the combination of an external device that suppor ts the ankle with a rehabilitation program, which includes early joint mobilization. Many functional treatment programs exi st, but it is not clear which program produces the best outcomes including preventing reinjury, and whether supervised exerc ises are superior to conventional treatment. Conventional treat ment includes all types of external support, which allow at most partial joint mobility, combined with standard non-supervis ed exercises, application of ice, and partial weight-bearing ( 30). Instead, this conventional treatment could be combined wit h"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_061", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 9, "page_end": 9, "char_count": 628, "text": "rehabilitation supervised by a physical therapist that usuall y begins within 2 weeks from the injury, and emphasizes balance training and coordination exercises. According to our revi ew, no strong evidence was found demonstrating the superiority o f supervised exercises to conventional treatment, especially if we consider outcome measures at short-term follow-up ( 27, 32). Restoring the ankle dorsiï¬‚exion of the ankle and preventing joint stiï¬€ness are key goals of rehabilitation programs becau se the loss of range of motion is a risk factor for recurrent sprai ns and chronic joint instability. Diï¬€erent interventions and"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_062", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 9, "page_end": 9, "char_count": 611, "text": "rehabilitation programs are proposed, including proprioceptive training, coordination training, strength training, func tional exercises, and manual mobilization. However, the most eï¬€ective method of ensuring such a restoration is yet to be determined ( 30). According to our review, the eï¬€ectiveness of manual therapy to improve ankle dorsiï¬‚exion seems to be limited to the early stages of rehabilitation ( 30). Its eï¬€ects have to be linked to increasing triceps surae ï¬‚exibility with static-stretching exercises, which could also be performed at home, decreasing the need for treatment in terms of time ( 29)."}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_063", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 9, "page_end": 9, "char_count": 614, "text": "However, at the same time, it is important to keep in mind that many factors contribute to limitations in ankle range o f motion, such as pain, muscle spasm, and swelling, so analgesics , myorelaxants, and anti-oedema drugs, as well as manipulativ e therapy and lymphatic drainage may also help to restore ankle dorsiï¬‚exion. Therefore, a multimodal approach is needed to establish which factors are reducing the range of motion in o rder to select the most appropriate interventions ( 28, 29). Motor control plays a key role in walking and feed forward postural mechanisms ( 8, 42, 43); however, following an ankle"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_064", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 9, "page_end": 9, "char_count": 632, "text": "sprain, lower limb muscle activation patterns may be altered ( 44), thus another popular intervention is neuromuscular training, particularly using the wobble balance board in the sub- acute phase, to improve proprioception that seems to prevent re-injury ( 26). Surgery Surgical treatment of acute ankle sprain generally consists of primary repair of torn ligaments with suturing. Kerkhoï¬€s et al. ( 33) analyzed trials comparing surgical interventions with conservative management and most of the evidence found was not suï¬ƒcient to demonstrate eï¬€ectiveness. However, they found some evidence that may demonstrate the superiority of"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_065", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 9, "page_end": 9, "char_count": 600, "text": "surgery to improve stability as objectively measured by a posit ive talar tilt or positive anterior drawer sign on stress radiogr aphs; nevertheless, the clinical relevance of these diï¬€erences re mains unclear. Moreover, surgery is associated with an increased r isk of stiï¬€ness and limitations in ankle mobility, higher costs, a nd additional complications associated with surgery itself, inc luding infection and deep vein thrombosis. It should be noted that ankle and foot surgery showed growing interest over the last decade ( 45, 46). Struijs and Kerkhoï¬€s ( 34) found that surgery is superior to"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_066", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 9, "page_end": 9, "char_count": 646, "text": "immobilization with respect to RTS and objectively decreasi ng instability. At the same time, however, they did not ï¬nd relevant diï¬€erences in clinical and functional outcome meas ures. Similarly, no diï¬€erences were found when surgery was compared with functional treatment. Due to the inconclusive and conï¬‚icting data about the eï¬ƒcacy and safety of surgical intervention, in our opinion, more stu dies are needed before recommending surgery as the ï¬rst option of treatment; however, we think that surgery should be consider ed when patients with persistent symptoms such as chronic ankle joint instability are non-responsive to other treatments."}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_067", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 9, "page_end": 9, "char_count": 647, "text": "Other Treatments Alternative medicine practices such as acupuncture and electrical stimulation have also been used to treat acute ankle sprains. Electrical stimulation is an electrical current produced fr om an external device that is applied to the body using an electrode. The electrical current can depolarize sensory or motor nerve ï¬bers, curb the formation of edema, and facilita te tissue healing. In theory, diï¬€erent types of electrical stimu lation could be used, such as high-voltage pulsed electrical stimul ation and neuromuscular electrical stimulation. High-voltage pu lsed electrical stimulation is used to suppress edema development"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_068", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 9, "page_end": 9, "char_count": 650, "text": "by modifying cell permeability. In contrast, neuromuscular electrical stimulation is used to facilitate muscle contrac tions, which in turn improves venous and lymphatic return and decreases edema. According to our revision, no evidence exi sts to support the eï¬€ectiveness of electrical stimulation in the management of acute ankle sprains ( 35). Concerning acupuncture, some studies would advocate its eï¬€ectiveness, especially as an add-on treatment during pain management in grade I and II ankle sprains; moreover, it seems to be more eï¬€ective than a placebo. Nevertheless, these result s are based on subjective parameters such as patient-reported"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_069", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 9, "page_end": 10, "char_count": 637, "text": "global symptom improvement or patient-reported functional assessment and studies with a few patients, so results can be Frontiers in Medicine | www.frontiersin.org 9 July 2022 | Volume 9 | Article 868474 Gaddi et al. Acute Ankle Sprain Management overestimated. Furthermore, no study demonstrates a statistically signiï¬cant result when acupuncture was compared with NSAIDs as a single treatment or add-on treatment. Moreover, almost a ll studies are conducted in China, where acupuncture is an ancie nt medical technique that is widely integrated into their cult ure and healthcare system, so further research is needed to establi sh the"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_070", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 10, "page_end": 10, "char_count": 616, "text": "acceptance of this practice in other countries ( 36, 37). Acupuncture may represent another pain management option to reduce the use of medications; therefore, more clinical t rials are required to examine the eï¬€ectiveness of acupuncture to tre at acute musculoskeletal injuries, especially as an add-on treatment, and to test the acceptance of this practice in cultures outside of China. Moreover, in our opinion, it will be important to analyze the eï¬ƒcacy of acupuncture using objective parameters and performance-based functional scores. Return to Sport Many athletes develop long-term symptoms, deï¬ned as chronic"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_071", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 10, "page_end": 10, "char_count": 610, "text": "ankle instability, and it is still debated whether early RTS plays a role in this. The key concerns with chronic ankle instability are the increased risk of reinjury and its seque lae such as post-traumatic osteoarthritis, instability, stiï¬€ne ss, muscle hypotonicity, pain, swelling, and functional loss and disabi lity ( 47, 48). The range of time to RTS is often based on the severity of the injury. A typical grade I lateral ankle sprain may take 2â€“ 6 weeks to RTS and a more severe syndesmotic injury takes an average of 46.4 days to RTS ( 40). According to our review, many studies separately investigat e"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_072", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 10, "page_end": 10, "char_count": 622, "text": "inï¬‚uencing factors for RTS, but they failed to determine a paradigm ( 38). The decision about RTS should include balance, proprioception, strength, range of motion, agility tests, and psychological stress. It is important to evaluate both intrin sic modiï¬able risk factors, such as balance, endurance, muscle strength, range of motion, and extrinsic risk factors, such as the playing surface or environmental conditions ( 39). An opinion shared by many experts is that a crucial element when determining an athleteâ€™s readiness for RTS is the need to ass ess sport-speciï¬c movements, and consequently, each practitione r is"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_073", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 10, "page_end": 10, "char_count": 606, "text": "encouraged to establish an objective scale to standardize l ateral ankle sprain RTS decisions ( 41). The idea that the time since the injury (trauma) is the primary criterion for determining RTS readiness has been overcome. In the same way, the grading of injuries based on physical exam ï¬ndings (e.g., special tests) or the histological damag e seen on diagnostic images is of limited value when determini ng RTS, especially considering the inherent diï¬€erence between these injuries and the diï¬€erent types of treatment athletes receive . A deï¬nition that uniï¬es these criticalities considers the RT S as a"}
{"chunk_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review__chunk_074", "document_id": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review", "filename": "06_PMC9301067_Acute_Ankle_Sprain_Umbrella_Review.pdf", "institution": "Frontiers in Medicine (indexed in PubMed Central)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9301067/", "document_date": "2022-07-07", "review_date": "not applicable", "page_start": 10, "page_end": 10, "char_count": 608, "text": "dynamic decision-making model, organized in three consecu tive phases, which are based on the recovery of each individual athlete. These three phases are identiï¬ed as ( 49). - â€œreturn to participation, â€ in which the athlete is allowed to train but not perform. - â€œreturn to sport, â€ in which the athlete performs but not at the levels desired before trauma. - â€œreturn to performance, â€ in which an athlete returns to thei r pre-injury level and which is considered the goal condition. Future studies should investigate the diï¬€erent variables th at could inï¬‚uence RTS and develop a decision paradigm based on"}
